# An application that can actually attempt an outbound request.
#
# Every other example here either assembles or prints a file. Phase 4.4 is
# about what an application may REACH, and that cannot be validated by a
# container with no way to try — so this one carries a wget and the CA bundle
# it needs to complete a TLS handshake, then sleeps so a runbook can exec into
# it and ask.
#
# curl, not busybox wget. The Phase 4.4 walk found busybox's wget cannot make
# an HTTPS request through an HTTP CONNECT proxy at all: it ignores https_proxy
# and HTTPS_PROXY outright, and when given http_proxy it issues a cleartext
# proxied GET, which FatLine correctly refuses. An application that cannot
# speak to the boundary cannot demonstrate anything about it.
#
# The trap is what lets it stop. The shell is PID 1, and PID 1 receives only
# the signals it has a handler for, so `sh -c "… && sleep N"` ignores SIGTERM
# and the Pod bills through its whole grace period — the 2026-11-10 walk saw it
# at the old pod count for three ticks after a stop. The trap installs a
# handler, and `wait` returns the moment a trapped signal arrives; `exec sleep`
# would not help, since sleep as PID 1 ignores SIGTERM just the same.
FROM alpine:3.19
RUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates curl
CMD ["sh", "-c", "echo egress-demo ready; trap 'exit 0' TERM INT; sleep 86400 & wait"]
